1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,530
Jeanne:

2
00:00:01,530 --> 00:00:04,530
Hi Thomas, how are you enjoying the course so far?

3
00:00:04,530 --> 00:00:05,124
Thomas:

4
00:00:05,124 --> 00:00:06,190
Yeah, I think it’s good.

5
00:00:06,190 --> 00:00:07,075
Jeanne:

6
00:00:07,075 --> 00:00:09,548
Remind me – why did you decide to study sports science?

7
00:00:09,548 --> 00:00:13,570
Didn’t you want to be a professional athlete when you were at school?

8
00:00:13,570 --> 00:00:13,620
Thomas:

9
00:00:13,620 --> 00:00:15,286
Yeah – that was my goal,

10
00:00:15,286 --> 00:00:17,860
and all my classmates assumed I would achieve it;

11
00:00:17,860 --> 00:00:19,290
they thought I was brilliant.

12
00:00:19,290 --> 00:00:19,340
Jeanne:

13
00:00:19,340 --> 00:00:21,360
That must have been a nice feeling.

14
00:00:21,360 --> 00:00:21,410
Thomas:

15
00:00:21,410 --> 00:00:23,782
Mm, I thought I could win anything.

16
00:00:23,782 --> 00:00:26,810
There was no one who could run faster than me.

17
00:00:26,810 --> 00:00:27,195
Jeanne:

18
00:00:27,195 --> 00:00:28,735
Exactly – so what happened?

19
00:00:28,735 --> 00:00:32,970
Did your mum and dad want you to be more ‘academic’?

20
00:00:32,970 --> 00:00:33,319
Thomas:

21
00:00:33,319 --> 00:00:34,367
Not at all.

22
00:00:34,367 --> 00:00:37,160
Perhaps they should have pushed me harder, though.

23
00:00:37,160 --> 00:00:37,386
Jeanne:

24
00:00:37,386 --> 00:00:38,290
What do you mean?

25
00:00:38,290 --> 00:00:38,588
Thomas:

26
00:00:38,588 --> 00:00:40,670
I think I should have practised more.

27
00:00:40,670 --> 00:00:40,917
Jeanne:

28
00:00:40,917 --> 00:00:42,150
What makes you say that?

29
00:00:42,150 --> 00:00:42,414
Thomas:

30
00:00:42,414 --> 00:00:45,850
Well, I went out to Kenya for a couple of weeks to train …

31
00:00:45,850 --> 00:00:46,224
Jeanne:

32
00:00:46,224 --> 00:00:46,599
Really!

33
00:00:46,599 --> 00:00:48,470
I didn’t know that.

34
00:00:48,470 --> 00:00:48,817
Thomas:

35
00:00:48,817 --> 00:00:52,634
I was chosen to go there out of loads of kids

36
00:00:52,634 --> 00:00:56,798
and run with some of the top teenage athletes in the world.

37
00:00:56,798 --> 00:00:59,227
And … I was so calm about it.

38
00:00:59,227 --> 00:01:02,004
I just kept thinking how fortunate I was.

39
00:01:02,004 --> 00:01:04,086
What a great chance this was!

40
00:01:04,086 --> 00:01:06,862
Everyone back home was so proud of me.

41
00:01:06,862 --> 00:01:11,720
But once we started competing, I very quickly realised I wasn’t good enough.

42
00:01:11,720 --> 00:01:12,055
Jeanne:

43
00:01:12,055 --> 00:01:14,400
That must have been a huge shock.

44
00:01:14,400 --> 00:01:14,725
Thomas:

45
00:01:14,725 --> 00:01:16,997
I thought ‘this can’t be happening’!

46
00:01:16,997 --> 00:01:18,620
I was used to winning.

47
00:01:18,620 --> 00:01:18,864
Jeanne:

48
00:01:18,864 --> 00:01:20,330
I’m sorry to hear that.

49
00:01:20,330 --> 00:01:20,625
Thomas:

50
00:01:20,625 --> 00:01:21,508
It’s OK.

51
00:01:21,508 --> 00:01:26,516
I’m over it now and I think it’s much better to do a university course

52
00:01:26,516 --> 00:01:29,462
and this one has such a variety of sports-related areas.

53
00:01:29,462 --> 00:01:31,230
It’s going to be good.

54
00:01:31,230 --> 00:01:31,830
Jeanne:

55
00:01:31,830 --> 00:01:37,230
Oh, I agree – I chose it because of that.

56
00:01:37,230 --> 00:01:37,557
Thomas:

57
00:01:37,557 --> 00:01:42,795
So Jeanne – have you thought of any ideas for the discussion session next week on technology

58
00:01:42,795 --> 00:01:43,450
and sport?

59
00:01:43,450 --> 00:01:43,718
Jeanne:

60
00:01:43,718 --> 00:01:46,660
We have to cover more than one sport, don’t we?

61
00:01:46,660 --> 00:01:47,005
Thomas:

62
00:01:47,005 --> 00:01:47,350
Yeah.

63
00:01:47,350 --> 00:01:47,741
Jeanne:

64
00:01:47,741 --> 00:01:51,655
You know – we always think technology is about the future,

65
00:01:51,655 --> 00:01:55,960
but we could gather some ideas about past developments in sport.

66
00:01:55,960 --> 00:01:56,370
Thomas:

67
00:01:56,370 --> 00:01:59,243
Look at early types of equipment perhaps?

68
00:01:59,243 --> 00:02:04,578
Uh, I remember reading something about table tennis bats once – how they ended

69
00:02:04,578 --> 00:02:07,040
up being covered with pimpled rubber.

70
00:02:07,040 --> 00:02:07,315
Jeanne:

71
00:02:07,315 --> 00:02:10,060
Cos they were just wooden at first, I’d imagine.

72
00:02:10,060 --> 00:02:10,440
Thomas:

73
00:02:10,440 --> 00:02:10,819
Yeah.

74
00:02:10,819 --> 00:02:16,132
In about the 1920s, a factory was making rolls of the rubber in bulk

75
00:02:16,132 --> 00:02:18,030
for something like horse harnesses.

76
00:02:18,030 --> 00:02:18,640
Jeanne:

77
00:02:18,640 --> 00:02:19,250
Really!

78
00:02:19,250 --> 00:02:19,561
Thomas:

79
00:02:19,561 --> 00:02:23,919
Yeah – and someone realised that it’d make a perfect covering for the wooden

80
00:02:23,919 --> 00:02:24,230
bats.

81
00:02:24,230 --> 00:02:24,647
Jeanne:

82
00:02:24,647 --> 00:02:29,240
So what about cricket – that’s had a few innovative changes.

83
00:02:29,240 --> 00:02:32,580
Maybe the pads they wear on their legs?

84
00:02:32,580 --> 00:02:32,916
Thomas:

85
00:02:32,916 --> 00:02:38,284
I don’t think they’ve changed much but, I’m just looking on the internet …

86
00:02:38,284 --> 00:02:42,981
and it says that when the first cricket helmet came in, in 1978, the

87
00:02:42,981 --> 00:02:47,342
Australian batsman who first wore it was booed and jeered by people watching

88
00:02:47,342 --> 00:02:49,020
because it was so ugly!

89
00:02:49,020 --> 00:02:49,474
Jeanne:

90
00:02:49,474 --> 00:02:53,558
Wow, players have to protect themselves from getting hurt!

91
00:02:53,558 --> 00:02:56,280
I mean everyone wears one now.

92
00:02:56,280 --> 00:02:56,765
Thomas:

93
00:02:56,765 --> 00:02:59,190
Mm, unlike the cycle helmet.

94
00:02:59,190 --> 00:02:59,555
Jeanne:

95
00:02:59,555 --> 00:03:01,743
Well, unless you’re a professional,

96
00:03:01,743 --> 00:03:06,120
but you’re right, many ordinary bikers don’t wear a helmet.

97
00:03:06,120 --> 00:03:06,544
Thomas:

98
00:03:06,544 --> 00:03:10,355
Hey, look at these pictures of original helmet designs.

99
00:03:10,355 --> 00:03:13,320
This one looks like an upside-down bowl!

100
00:03:13,320 --> 00:03:13,696
Jeanne:

101
00:03:13,696 --> 00:03:18,580
Yet, the woman’s laughing – she’s so proud to be wearing it!

102
00:03:18,580 --> 00:03:18,943
Thomas:

103
00:03:18,943 --> 00:03:24,030
It says serious cyclists ended up with wet hair from all the hard exercise.

104
00:03:24,030 --> 00:03:24,312
Jeanne:

105
00:03:24,312 --> 00:03:27,982
I guess that’s why they have large air vents in them now

106
00:03:27,982 --> 00:03:30,240
so that the skin can breathe more easily.

107
00:03:30,240 --> 00:03:30,687
Thomas:

108
00:03:30,687 --> 00:03:31,133
OK,

109
00:03:31,133 --> 00:03:33,367
so we’ve done helmets.

110
00:03:33,367 --> 00:03:39,620
What about golf balls or better still golf clubs – they’ve changed a lot.

111
00:03:39,620 --> 00:03:39,978
Jeanne:

112
00:03:39,978 --> 00:03:43,199
Yeah – I remember my great grandfather telling me that

113
00:03:43,199 --> 00:03:47,493
because a club was made entirely of wood, it would easily break

114
00:03:47,493 --> 00:03:49,640
and players had to get another.

115
00:03:49,640 --> 00:03:49,846
Thomas:

116
00:03:49,846 --> 00:03:52,110
There’s no wood at all in them now, is there?

117
00:03:52,110 --> 00:03:52,456
Jeanne:

118
00:03:52,456 --> 00:03:54,530
No – they’re much more powerful.

119
00:03:54,530 --> 00:03:54,799
Thomas:

120
00:03:54,799 --> 00:03:56,950
The same must be true of hockey sticks.

121
00:03:56,950 --> 00:03:57,372
Jeanne:

122
00:03:57,372 --> 00:04:02,434
I don’t think so because players still use wooden sticks today.

123
00:04:02,434 --> 00:04:08,341
What it does say here, though, is that when the game started you had

124
00:04:08,341 --> 00:04:10,450
to produce a stick yourself.

125
00:04:10,450 --> 00:04:10,918
Thomas:

126
00:04:10,918 --> 00:04:14,658
I guess they just weren’t being manufactured.

127
00:04:14,658 --> 00:04:16,528
So, one more perhaps.

128
00:04:16,528 --> 00:04:17,930
What about football?

129
00:04:17,930 --> 00:04:18,302
Jeanne:

130
00:04:18,302 --> 00:04:22,400
Well, I know the first balls were made of animal skin.

131
00:04:22,400 --> 00:04:22,753
Thomas:

132
00:04:22,753 --> 00:04:26,991
Yeah, they covered them with pieces of leather that were stitched together,

133
00:04:26,991 --> 00:04:30,170
but … the balls let in water when it rained.

134
00:04:30,170 --> 00:04:30,469
Jeanne:

135
00:04:30,469 --> 00:04:30,768
Oh,

136
00:04:30,768 --> 00:04:32,860
that would have made them much heavier.

137
00:04:32,860 --> 00:04:33,168
Thomas:

138
00:04:33,168 --> 00:04:34,093
That’s right.

139
00:04:34,093 --> 00:04:38,100
You can imagine the damage to players’ necks when the ball was headed.

140
00:04:38,100 --> 00:04:38,404
Jeanne:

141
00:04:38,404 --> 00:04:40,230
How painful that must have been!

142
00:04:40,230 --> 00:04:40,711
Thomas:

143
00:04:40,711 --> 00:04:46,000
Yeah, well, I think we can put together some useful ideas …
